#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# symmetric_reduction

#% text
# `symmetric_reduction(f, [x1, ..., xn])` gives `[p, q]` with `f = p + q`, p a polynomial in the elementary symmetric polynomials of the xi and q the remainder.

#% text
# `symmetric_reduction(f, xs, [s1, ..., sn])` writes p in the names si for the elementary symmetric polynomials.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The leading term in the lexicographic order of xs is reduced by the fundamental theorem of symmetric polynomials when its exponents do not increase, else moved to q; q is 0 exactly when f is symmetric.

#% item
# Factors free of the xi are coefficients.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
symmetric_reduction(x^2 + y^2, [x, y])
#% output
#= [-2*x*y + (x + y)^2, 0]

#% cell
symmetric_reduction(x^2 - y^2, [x, y], [s1, s2])
#% output
#= [-2*s2 + s1^2, -2*y^2]

#% cell
symmetric_reduction(x^2 - y^2, [y, x])
#% output
#= [-(x + y)^2 + 2*x*y, 2*x^2]

#% cell
symmetric_reduction(x^3 + y^3 + z^3, [x, y, z], [s1, s2, s3])
#% output
#= [3*s3 - 3*s1*s2 + s1^3, 0]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `symmetric_polynomial`, `polynomial_reduce`
